          "image_source": "images/page_16_chart_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.597,
            0.223,
            0.666,
            0.276
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_16_image_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "chart",
          "bbox": [
            0.675,
            0.221,
            0.859,
            0.274
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.753,
                0.277,
                0.776,
                0.291
              ],
              "content": "(b)"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_16_chart_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.186,
            0.278,
            0.587,
            0.37
          ],
          "content": "Even when different instruments like a flute, ektara, or tabla play the same note at the same loudness each sounds unique. This quality is called timbre, and comes from their shape, material and construction, which determine the pattern and intensity of the overtones."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.594,
            0.298,
            0.866,
            0.356
          ],
          "content": "Fig. 10.25: Schematic representation of the sound produced by (a) a vibrating tuning fork or a person whistling, and (b) a child singing"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.187,
            0.369,
            0.87,
            0.417
          ],
          "content": "An octave is the interval between two notes where one has double the frequency of the other (for example, 200 Hz and 400 Hz), measured between their fundamental frequencies."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.191,
            0.44,
            0.358,
            0.458
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Meet a Scientist"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.199,
            0.475,
            0.694,
            0.555
          ],
          "content": "**Sir C. V. Raman** won India’s first Nobel Prize in Science for discovering the Raman Effect in light. He also made important contributions to acoustics by studying Indian percussion instruments, such as the tabla and mridangam to understand how they produce rich and nuanced sounds."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.716,
            0.461,
            0.839,
            0.552
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_16_image_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
