              "content": "Fig. 10.26: A tabla set"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_16_image_16.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.225,
            0.766,
            0.536,
            0.787
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "10.7 Reflection of Sound"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.146,
            0.794,
            0.211,
            0.826
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Grade 8 Curiosity Chapter 10"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.222,
            0.79,
            0.89,
            0.91
          ],
          "content": "Sound waves can bounce off obstacles like solids or liquids and this is known as the **reflection of sound**. Sound follow the same laws of reflection as those of light which you have learned earlier. The directions in which the sound is incident and is thereafter reflected make equal angles with the normal to the reflecting surface at the point of incidence, with all three lying in the same plane. One of the most common examples of the reflection of sound is an echo."
        },
        {
